{
 "id": "TD-11",
 "system": "ac3",
 "sessionId": "bm-ac2-td-11-27537a77f3",
 "endReason": "turn-limit",
 "customerTurns": 8,
 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "tumble dryer not working"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is clean the lint/fluff filter (and condenser filter) and clear the airflow. A blocked lint filter stops the airflow, and many dryers then cut the heat to stop overheating. Clean the fluff filter in the door opening (heat-pump models often have a second one at the bottom too). Was it full of fluff (and is it clean now), or was it already clean?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Haven't checked the filter yet. Lights come on but nothing happens when I press start, no sound at all. Door's felt loose for a few days too."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is clean the lint/fluff filter. Can you hear/feel the fan moving air, and is it clear of fluff?\n[Helpful to see: image \"Where the lint filter is\" — The fluff/lint filter sits in the door opening or just inside the rim. Lift it out and clear the fluff after every load. Typical layout — your dryer may differ.]"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Not sure how to check the fan. There's no sound at all when I press start. The door doesn't click shut either, it swings back slightly open. Could that be it?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "It could be, as a blocked filter can cause overheating and safety cut-offs, but since there is no sound at all when you press start, the issue likely lies with the power supply or the door switch rather than just airflow. You should check if the machine receives power by plugging it into a different socket and ensure the door latch is engaging properly given the loose feel you mentioned. From what you have described, the most likely cause is the door catch / door switch. With the dryer unplugged, look at the catch on the door and the slot it clicks into: fluff or a bit of fabric can stop it engaging, and check the door lines up when you close it. Is the catch broken, was something stopping it (now cleared), or does it all look fine?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Yes it's broken. The plastic tongue on the door has snapped off, found the piece on the floor. If I hold the door shut and press start it runs, stops when I let go."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is door handle / catch (mechanical). Is the handle/door physically broken or not closing, or does it close fine but the dryer still says the door is open?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "The door doesn't close properly, it swings back slightly open because the plastic tongue snapped off. Does that mean I need a new part?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "[Heading: Likely cause: door handle]\nYes, the broken plastic tongue on the door catch needs to be replaced for the machine to operate safely. You will likely need a new door handle or catch assembly depending on how it is attached to your specific model. The checks point to the door handle. To find the right part, could you give me the make and model number? It is usually on a sticker inside the door opening.\n[Panel: \"To check a suitable replacement — Enter your model number below. Once we have the model I can check whether a suitable replacement is available. It's usually on a sticker inside the door or around the frame, or on the back of the appliance.\"]"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "It's a Hotpoint. I don't know the model offhand, I'll go and look inside the door recess and tell you."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "[Heading: Likely cause: door handle]\nThe checks point to the door handle. To find the right part, could you give me the model number? It is usually on a sticker inside the door opening.\n[Panel: \"To check a suitable replacement — Enter your model number below. Once we have the model I can check whether a suitable replacement is available. It's usually on a sticker inside the door or around the frame, or on the back of the appliance.\"]"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "ok, just been to look. I can't see a model number on the sticker I'm afraid, it's a bit worn. Is it fine if I just go off it being a Hotpoint?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "[Heading: Likely cause: door handle]\nNo, you cannot rely solely on the brand because Hotpoint tumble dryers use different internal components and part numbers depending on the specific model. You must find the full model number to identify the correct replacement door catch or any other parts needed for repair. Open the door, make sure no clothing is caught in it, then shut it firmly until it clicks and press start. Does it still show the door as open?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "It won't click shut, the door just swings back slightly open because the tongue is snapped. If I hold it firmly shut and press start it runs, but it stops as soon as I let go."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "[Heading: Likely cause: door handle]\nDoes a timed programme dry fine while only the AutoDry/sensor programme misbehaves, and does it end too early or never finish?"
  }
 ],
